---
import SlotBlocked022 from "../../../components/studies/SlotBlocked022.svelte";
import SlotOutsideRules021 from "../../../components/studies/SlotOutsideRules021.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "ship-slots-2026-11-11");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A slot for a part outside the scored rules</h2>
    <p>
      In <a href="/hangar">the Hangar</a>'s Plan view every mount on the ship
      drawing has a slot: a marked rectangle that shows what the mount accepts,
      whether it is filled and whether it is selected. A filled slot has a lime
      border and a lime type label on a dark fill.
    </p>
    <p>
      Playground is the Hangar setting that allows parts outside the scored
      rules. With Playground on, such a part can be fitted and the run is
      unscored. With Playground off, a fitted one stays on the ship and blocks
      every launch. In both cases its slot looks like any other filled slot. The
      launch buttons are unavailable with no reason given, and the player finds
      the part by selecting mounts one at a time and reading each mount's part
      list.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lime border and label already mean fitted, and the new state has to be
      readable in the same rectangle. The Fiddler, the ship with the most slots,
      has nine. A slot marks where a part is placed, and the part can reach
      beyond it; Plan view today shrinks weapons and utilities to fit inside the
      slot, which is a defect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fixed: the slot's geometry; its empty, filled and selected states; slots
      drawn only in Plan view; Playground's behaviour; and the linked mount, the
      second engine of a mirrored pair, which copies the first and cannot be
      selected. Open: what a filled slot shows when its part is outside the
      scored rules, with Playground on and with it off, selected and not, and on
      a linked mount. It is judged on the whole Fiddler. The answer changes the
      slot drawing that the Hangar and the Lab's mount tools share. Out of
      scope:
      <a href="/lab/studies/part-rows-2026-11-11">the rows of the part list</a>,
      <a href="/lab/studies/run-status-2026-11-10"
        >what parts outside the scored rules are called</a
      >, a ship that is itself outside the scored rules, and repairing that
      defect.
    </p>

    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 11 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        An independent reviewer with no part in the framing tested the first
        draft of this section against the question rubric. Two claims were
        false. The section was then cut to a third of its length, corrected for
        every finding and edited for plain wording; this text has not been
        tested again.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/ship-slots-2026-11-11">Full result and findings</a
        >.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">Stamp</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              One object, the slot, gaining a state for a part outside the
              scored rules; its conditions are facets one example shows.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Fail</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists, but the draft counted eight slots where
              the Fiddler has nine, and said a ship's tab shows its class, which
              it does not with Playground on.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              A slot shows when its part is outside the scored rules, so a ship
              that cannot launch no longer looks ready.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Marking the slot on a whole Fiddler</h2>
    <h3 id="02-1">02.1 / A corner for the class, red for forbidden</h3>
    <p>
      The first answer treats the two conditions as one fact at two strengths. A
      slot whose part is outside the scored rules gets a filled corner. With
      Playground on the corner is caution yellow and the slot stays lime: the
      part is fitted and allowed, and the corner is information. With Playground
      off the corner is red and red replaces lime in the border, the type label
      and the pair's connector, because lime there would say the part is fine.
      Selection corners stay lime in both.
    </p>
    <SlotOutsideRules021 client:load />
    <p>
      Settled on 11 November 2026: a part outside the scored rules is marked by
      a filled corner in the colour of its slot's border, yellow on the
      Playground-on ship and red on the Playground-off ship. A corner triangle
      is a stock web pattern, which the game otherwise avoids; it is accepted
      here. Not settled: the red slot does not yet identify the part that blocks
      launch.
    </p>
    <h3 id="02-2">02.2 / A heavier border on the slot that blocks launch</h3>
    <p>
      The corner and its colour rule are kept. One thing changes: with
      Playground off, the red border of a blocking slot is drawn at the weight
      of the selection corners, dashed on the linked engine. In 02.1 that border
      was the slot's thinnest line. The ships are drawn larger than in 02.1,
      nearer the Hangar's size.
    </p>
    <SlotBlocked022 client:load />
    <p>
      To notice: whether the three blocking slots are the first thing seen on
      the right-hand ship, and whether a selected blocking slot, lime corners
      around a red border, still reads as selected. The heavy border is drawn
      over the part and would cross a barrel that reaches beyond the slot. The
      selection glow on a blocked engine is still lime.
    </p>
    <h3 id="stopped">Stopped on 11 November 2026</h3>
    <p>
      The study was stopped as inconclusive before 02.2 was judged. The
      exploration had stopped advancing, and the slot needs a language that does
      not exist yet: how the game marks a part outside the scored rules at all.
      That language is to be developed first in the
      <a href="/lab/studies/part-rows-2026-11-11">part-rows study</a>. What
      remains from this study: the question in 01 and its
      <a href="/lab/reviews/ship-slots-2026-11-11">review</a>; the note under
      02.1; and two observations from the examples, that the slot's border is
      too thin to carry a state alone and that the parts cover the slot's fill.
      02.2 is an unjudged proposal. The closure record is
      <code>docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-11-ship-slots-study.md</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
/* The prose column's width; the stamp's own type is smaller. */
.stamp {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: 41rem;
  margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
.stamp h3 {
  margin-top: 0;
}
.stamp table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}
.stamp th,
.stamp td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
.stamp td:not(:last-child) {
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.stamp tbody th {
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-weight: normal;
  color: var(--color-text);
}
</style>
